        <div id="efficiency" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Are Infrared Heaters More Efficient? No, but They Feel Warmer Faster
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the one fact most infrared heater listings blur, so it is worth getting straight before you spend anything. Every plug-in electric heater, whatever it is called, turns close to all of the electricity it draws into heat. There is nowhere else for the energy to go. A heater rated at 1,500 watts produces about 5,100 BTU an hour, which is why so many listings quote 5,100 or 5,200 BTU. That number is not a feature of one brand; it is simply what 1,500 watts is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Every 1,500-Watt Heater Makes the Same Heat</h3>

          <p>
            A ceramic fan heater, an oil-filled radiator, a mica panel and an infrared quartz heater, all set to 1,500 watts, put the same amount of heat into your house each hour and add the same amount to your electricity bill. When a listing promises that its heater gives 60 percent more warmth than a standard 1,500-watt heater, it cannot mean more heat energy. At best it means you feel warmer sooner, because of how the heat reaches you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Infrared Does Differently</h3>

          <p>
            Convection heaters warm the air, and the warm air then rises to the ceiling and slowly circulates down to you. Infrared heaters send radiant energy in a straight line, and it turns into heat when it hits a surface: your skin, your clothes, the sofa, the floor. That is why you feel a quartz heater the moment it glows, even in a cold room, and why the warmth fades quickly if you step out of its beam. It heats what it can see.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where That Difference Saves Money</h3>

          <p>
            Infrared can lower your bill in one honest way: by letting you be comfortable while the rest of the house stays cooler. If you sit at a desk or on one end of the sofa, an infrared heater aimed at you can keep you warm on its low setting, and you can turn the central thermostat down a few degrees. The savings come from heating less space, not from a more efficient heater. Any space heater used this way can do the same; infrared is just quicker to feel.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Honest Truth</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Same watts, same heat, same bill.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Infrared is a delivery method, not a more efficient heater. Buy it because you want fast warmth where you sit, not because the box says it will cut your bill.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

