        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best infrared heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the Dr. Infrared DR-978 is the best infrared heater: a UL and cUL certified cabinet heater with tip-over and overheat shut-off, enclosed elements, a thermostat from 50 to 86 degrees and a 12-hour timer. The Comfort Zone CZ2062C is the best value cabinet, and the BLACK+DECKER BHTI06 is the cheapest way to warm a desk or one chair.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are infrared heaters more energy efficient than other space heaters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Every plug-in electric heater turns close to all its electricity into heat, so a 1,500-watt infrared heater makes the same heat as a 1,500-watt ceramic or oil-filled heater. Infrared feels warmer sooner because it heats you and nearby objects directly. It saves money only if that lets you turn the central heating down and warm just one room.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run an infrared heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 1,500-watt infrared heater on high uses 1.5 kilowatt-hours per hour. At 17 cents per kilowatt-hour that is about 26 cents an hour, or about $2 for eight hours at full power. The low setting halves that, and a thermostat cuts it further by switching the heater off when the room is warm. Check your bill for your own rate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to leave an infrared heater on overnight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is safer not to. Even with tip-over and overheat shut-off, a heater running unattended near bedding is a fire risk. If you use one in a bedroom, keep three feet clear, plug it into a wall outlet and set the timer to switch it off after you fall asleep. An electric blanket is a safer way to stay warm through the night.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size infrared heater do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Nearly all plug-in infrared heaters are 1,500 watts, the most a standard outlet supplies. As a rule of thumb, allow about 10 watts per square foot when the heater is the only heat source, so one heater fully heats about 150 square feet. In larger rooms it works as supplemental heat for the area where you sit, alongside your central heating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do infrared heaters dry out the air?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Infrared heaters do not remove moisture from the air, but neither does any other electric heater. Air feels drier in winter because warming it lowers its relative humidity, and that happens whatever heats the room. If dry air bothers you, run a humidifier, or choose a heater with a built-in one such as the Dr. Infrared DR-998.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use an infrared heater in a garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A plug-in 1,500-watt infrared heater can keep you warm at a workbench if you aim it at where you stand, but it cannot heat a whole garage in winter. For that you need a hardwired 240-volt electric heater or a properly vented gas unit. Keep it away from fuel, solvents and sawdust, and plug it directly into a wall outlet.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

